@prefix ldh:    <https://w3id.org/atomgraph/linkeddatahub#> .
@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix dh:     <https://www.w3.org/ns/ldt/document-hierarchy#> .
@prefix dct:    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<> a dh:Item ;
    dct:title "Import RDF data" ;
    dct:description "Step-by-step guide to importing RDF data" ;
    rdf:_1 <#content> .

<#content> a ldh:XHTML ;
    rdf:value """<div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
    <p class="lead">Step-by-step guide to importing RDF data</p>
    <div class="alert alert-info">
        <p>This guide is for importing larger amounts of data (e.g. more than a few thousand RDF triples) asynchronously.
        For smaller data, it is simpler to <a href="../../add-data/" target="_blank">add data</a> synchronously.</p>
    </div>
    <p>An RDF import has these components:</p>
    <dl>
        <dt>RDF file</dt>
        <dd>The file containing the RDF data (can be in any RDF syntax)</dd>
        <dt>Transformation query (optional)</dt>
        <dd>SPARQL <code>CONSTRUCT</code> query that maps RDF to another RDF representation</dd>
        <dt>Graph (optional)</dt>
        <dd>The document that the import results will be stored in, if no transformation query is specified</dd>
        <dt>RDF import</dt>
        <dd>Combines the file with the corresponding query</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="alert alert-info">
        <p>Specify either a query or a graph, but not both.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h2 id="import-rdf-file">Import an RDF file</h2>
        <p>Without a transformation, the import <em>asynchronously</em> appends the RDF data to a document (see <a href="../../create-data/create-documents/">how to create a document</a>).
            With a transformation, it runs the uploaded RDF through a <code>CONSTRUCT</code> query before storing it, so the import can restructure or re-route the data.</p>
        <div class="ldhc-tabs or-horizontal">
            <div class="ldhc-tablist sz-md va-line" role="tablist">
                <button aria-controls="user-interface-pane" aria-selected="true" class="ldhc-tab is-on" id="user-interface-tab" role="tab" type="button"><span class="ldhc-tab-lbl">User interface</span></button>
                <button aria-controls="command-line-interface-pane" aria-selected="false" class="ldhc-tab" id="command-line-interface-tab" role="tab" type="button"><span class="ldhc-tab-lbl">Command line interface</span></button>
            </div>
            <div aria-labelledby="user-interface-tab" class="ldhc-tabpanel" id="user-interface-pane" role="tabpanel">
                    <div>
                        <p>All the import components can be created from a single dialog, but the steps below create them one at a time:</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>First, create a container for the data to be imported. This will be the import's <dfn>target container</dfn>.</li>
                            <li>Upload an RDF file. Click on the <span class="btn btn-primary create-action">Create <span class="caret"></span></span> dropdown in the top-left corner and select
                                    <dfn>File</dfn>. This will open a new form.</li>
                            <li>Fill out the <dfn>Item</dfn> fields — the document's <samp>Title</samp> (required) and optional <samp>Description</samp>, the same fields used when <a href="../../create-data/create-documents/">creating a document</a>.</li>
                            <li>
                                Fill out the mandatory <dfn>File</dfn> fields:
                                <dl>
                                    <dt>Filename</dt>
                                    <dd>Click on <dfn>Choose file</dfn> and select an RDF file from your computer</dd>
                                    <dt>Title</dt>
                                    <dd>File title</dd>
                                </dl>
                            </li>
                            <li>Once all the fields have been filled in, click <span class="btn btn-primary btn-save">Save</span>.</li>
                            <li>The browser will open your uploaded file.</li>
                            <li>
                                If you are importing with a transformation, create a <a href="../../../reference/imports/rdf/#transformation">transformation query</a> that will
                                transform the uploaded RDF to a new RDF structure. Click on the <span class="btn btn-primary create-action">Create <span class="caret"></span></span> dropdown in the top-left corner and
                                select <dfn>CONSTRUCT</dfn>. This will open a new form. Fill out the <samp>Item</samp> fields and the mandatory <dfn>CONSTRUCT</dfn> fields:
                                <dl>
                                    <dt>Text</dt>
                                    <dd>SPARQL <code>CONSTRUCT</code> query string</dd>
                                    <dd><em>You will not be able to save the query if its syntax is not valid SPARQL 1.1.</em></dd>
                                    <dt>Title</dt>
                                    <dd>Query title</dd>
                                </dl>
                                Once all the fields have been filled in, click <span class="btn btn-primary btn-save">Save</span>. You can find the query saved in the
                                <dfn>Queries</dfn> container, which you can open from the <a href="../../../reference/user-interface/#document-hierarchy">document tree</a> in the left sidebar.
                            </li>
                            <li>Click on the <span class="btn btn-primary create-action">Create <span class="caret"></span></span> dropdown button in the top-left corner and select <dfn>RDF Import</dfn>.
                                    This will open a new form.</li>
                            <li>Fill out the <samp>Item</samp> fields.</li>
                            <li>
                                Fill out the mandatory <dfn>RDF Import</dfn> fields:
                                <dl>
                                    <dt>File</dt>
                                    <dd>Start typing the name of your RDF file and select it from the typeahead</dd>
                                    <dt>Title</dt>
                                    <dd>Import title</dd>
                                </dl>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Select either the target graph or the transformation query — but not both:
                                <dl>
                                    <dt>Name</dt>
                                    <dd>Select the target graph (document) using the typeahead</dd>
                                    <dt>Query</dt>
                                    <dd>Select the transformation query using the typeahead</dd>
                                </dl>
                            </li>
                            <li>Click <span class="btn btn-primary btn-save">Save</span> and you will be redirected to the newly created import document.</li>
                            <li>Reload the document after a few moments to see the import progress. On success, the amount of data imported is
                                    displayed; on failure, the constraint violations or other import errors.</li>
                            <li>Go back to the target document to see the newly imported data items.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                </div>
            <div aria-labelledby="command-line-interface-tab" class="ldhc-tabpanel" hidden="hidden" id="command-line-interface-pane" role="tabpanel">
                    <div>
                        <p>The import is described by a document, which the command takes as its last argument, so create that document first. With the <a href="../../../reference/command-line-interface/">CLI</a> installed and the <samp>LDH_*</samp> variables set:</p>
                        <pre>doc=$(ldh create-item \\
    --title "UNESCO category mappings" \\
    --container "${LDH_BASE}categories/")

ldh imports import-rdf \\
    --title "UNESCO category mappings" \\
    --rdf-file categories/unesco-mappings.ttl \\
    --content-type "text/turtle" \\
    --graph "${LDH_BASE}categories/mappings/" \\
    "$doc"</pre>
                        <p>Pass either <code>--graph</code> with the target document's URI or <code>--query-file</code> with the path to a transformation query, but not both.
                            In the Northwind demo, the <a href="../../../reference/imports/rdf/#transformation-example">transformation query</a> routes each mapping into its category's own document.</p>
                        <p class="exhibit-links">Source: <a href="https://github.com/AtomGraph/LinkedDataHub-Apps/blob/master/demo/northwind-traders/categories/unesco-mappings.ttl" target="_blank">categories/unesco-mappings.ttl</a> — the mapping file that connects Northwind categories to UNESCO Thesaurus concepts, shown in full in the <a href="../../../reference/imports/rdf/">RDF imports reference</a></p>
                    </div>
                </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="lead"><a href="../../../reference/imports/rdf/" target="_blank">Read more about how RDF imports work.</a></p>
</div>"""^^rdf:XMLLiteral .
